[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Zero-Knowledge Rollups: Validity Proofs, zk-SNARKs, and zk-STARKs"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Zero-Knowledge Rollups (ZK-rollups) represent a cornerstone scaling solution for blockchain networks like Ethereum. By executing transactions off-chain and posting cryptographic validity proofs back to the main chain, ZK-rollups dramatically increase throughput while retaining the security guarantees of Layer 1."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Core Architecture of ZK-Rollups"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"At a high level, a ZK-rollup consists of two primary components: an off-chain virtual machine where transactions are processed and state changes are computed, and on-chain smart contracts that store the state root and verify cryptographic proofs."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Sequencers: Entities that collect user transactions, execute them off-chain, and publish batches to the Layer 1 contract."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Provers: Heavy computational nodes that generate validity proofs confirming the correct state transition of the rollup."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Verifiers: On-chain smart contracts that mathematically validate the proof before accepting the new state root."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Validity Proofs vs. Fraud Proofs"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Unlike Optimistic Rollups which rely on fraud proofs and a challenge period (typically 7 days), ZK-rollups utilize validity proofs. A validity proof guarantees the absolute correctness of a state transition the exact moment it is submitted to the chain, eliminating withdrawal delays."},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"zk-SNARKs vs. zk-STARKs"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"The two predominant cryptographic proof systems used in ZK-rollups are zk-SNARKs (Succinct Non-Interactive Arguments of Knowledge) and zk-STARKs (Scalable Transparent Arguments of Knowledge)."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"zk-SNARKs: Highly succinct proofs with small size and fast verification times. However, most traditional SNARK implementations require a trusted setup ceremony."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"zk-STARKs: Transparent proof systems that do not require a trusted setup and are quantum-resistant, though they typically result in larger proof sizes."},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Implementing a Basic Verifier Contract in Solidity"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Below is an example of a simplified Solidity smart contract interface that verifies a cryptographic proof associated with a state update."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"pragma solidity ^0.8.20;

interface IVerifier {
    function verifyProof(
        bytes calldata proof,
        uint256[] calldata publicInputs
    ) external view returns (bool);
}

contract RollupInbox {
    IVerifier public immutable verifier;
    bytes32 public stateRoot;

    event StateUpdated(bytes32 indexed newStateRoot);

    constructor(address _verifierAddress) {
        verifier = IVerifier(_verifierAddress);
    }

    function updateState(
        bytes32 _newStateRoot,
        bytes calldata _proof,
        uint256[] calldata _publicInputs
    ) external {
        bool isValid = verifier.verifyProof(_proof, _publicInputs);
        require(isValid, "Invalid validity proof");

        stateRoot = _newStateRoot;
        emit StateUpdated(_newStateRoot);
    }
}"},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"Zero-Knowledge Rollups bridge the gap between blockchain scalability and cryptographic security. As proving times decrease and hardware acceleration improves, zk-SNARKs and zk-STARKs will continue to drive the evolution of Web3 infrastructure."}]
